# Line endings are decided here rather than by whatever `core.autocrlf` each machine has.
#
# The repository has always stored LF, but a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`
# hands the working tree CRLF, and Prettier then reports a difference in dozens of files
# nobody has touched. A formatting check is only worth having when its answer does not
# depend on who ran it, so the working tree gets LF everywhere and Git stops translating.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The Gradle launcher Windows actually runs. A batch file wants CRLF; old versions of
# cmd.exe mis-read a bare LF, and this one is generated by Gradle rather than written here.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Left untouched byte for byte. Git guesses at most of these correctly on its own, but the
# keystore and the JAR are worth stating outright: a translated byte makes them unusable.
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text
*.jar binary
*.keystore binary
*.apk binary
